{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And I will come down and speak with you there, and I will take</b> – The subject of the verse is to say that at the time of the speaking with Moses the act of taking (אֲצִילוּת) will occur, and from Him it comes. And so it says in the account of the event (Numbers 11:25): \"And YHWH came down in the cloud and spoke to him.\" And Scripture did not specify what this speaking was, as is the case throughout the Torah, where it afterward explains what YHWH said and what He spoke, for the matter here is that the elders of whom it says that they prophesied did not hear speech from the mouth of HaShem, nor did He appear to them in a vision or in a dream. Rather, HaShem speaks with Moses, and from the spirit of Moses they know that prophecy. And this is the meaning of \"and they prophesied, and did not continue\" (ibid.), that they did not continue to prophesy of themselves, but in the prophecy that HaShem spoke to Moses — in that alone they prophesied.\nAnd this is the meaning of <b>וְאָצַלְתִּי</b>, that I will set aside for Myself, from the spirit that I have placed upon you, and I will place it upon them. And this is not like the expression \"and you shall place of your majesty upon him\" (Numbers 27:20), for אֲצִילוּת everywhere means withholding/retaining beside oneself, as in the expression \"and whatever my eyes desired I did not withhold from them\" (Ecclesiastes 2:10), \"have you not reserved for me a blessing?\" (Genesis 27:36), and likewise \"therefore they set apart from the lower chambers and from the middle ones from the ground\" (Ezekiel 42:6) — that there remained beside them, of the lower and middle chambers from the ground, so that the galleries would not consume from them in the upper ones. And likewise \"and unto the nobles of the children of Israel\" (Exodus 24:11) — those who remain and are distinguished from among them, who withdraw (נֶאֱצָלִים) to themselves from the totality of the multitude of the people; or they are so called because they are the great ones to whom all come.\nAnd the translators from other languages said \"אֲצִילוּת\" concerning the emanation of power from the powers of the Creator, extending to the created being, and they say of the soul \"emanated from the holy spirit,\" for with them it is a term of drawing down. But this is not correct in my view, for אֲצִילוּת would then mean drawing down, or placing, or separating, on the part of the giver himself.\nBut Onkelos (Onkelos on Numbers 11:17) does two things with it: he translates here \"וַאֲרַבֵּי\" (and I will multiply/increase), and likewise \"וְאֶל אֲצִילֵי\" — \"וּלְרַבְרְבֵי\" (and to the great ones), but he translates \"הֲלֹא אָצַלְתָּ לִי בְרָכָה\" — \"שְׁבַקְתְּ\" (you left/reserved). It appears that his view of אֲצִילוּת is that it is the concept of drawing down — the giver drawing down upon the recipient — and he says here: I will draw down from that which is beside you of the spirit of prophecy, and I will place it upon them. And further, the term is used of a drawing down that the giver draws to himself from a thing already given, and it remains with him, and this is the meaning of \"הֲלֹא אָצַלְתָּ לִי בְרָכָה\" — you drew down for me, to yourself, one of the blessings, to bless me with it.\nAnd this is the general principle regarding the elders: their prophecy is only from the spirit that speaks in Moses, and from him it comes to them. Therefore they said in Midrash Bamidbar Rabbah (end of BeHa'alotekha): A parable of a king who handed over an orchard to a watchman and gave him the wage of the watching. After a time the watchman said to him: I cannot guard it all by myself; bring others who will guard with me. The king said to him: I gave you my orchard to guard, and all the fruit of its guarding I gave to you, and now you say, bring others who will guard with me? I will bring others who will guard with you, but know that I am not giving them a wage of watching from what is mine; rather, out of the wage that I gave you, from there they take their wage. So said the Holy One, blessed be He, to Moses: I gave you spirit and knowledge to shepherd My children, and I did not seek another, so that you alone would be distinguished in that watching; and now you seek another? Know that from what is Mine they take nothing at all, but rather \"וְאָצַלְתִּי מִן הָרוּחַ אֲשֶׁר עָלֶיךָ וְשַׂמְתִּי עֲלֵיהֶם.\" And even so, Moses lacked nothing at all. Thus far the midrash.\nAnd what seems correct to me is that all the days of the elders this was done for them, that they would know everything that HaShem would command Israel through Moses in the needs of their time and the events that would come upon them in the wilderness. And this is the meaning of \"and they shall bear with you in the burden of the people,\" for everything that Moses would say to the people, they would know, and each one would prophesy them to the people in his tribe and his thousand, and because of this Moses alone would not bear their complaints."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:04:47.165411115Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}